  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "myopia-related-2796",
  "summary": "Public transcriptomic and proteomic cohorts are increasingly combined with machine learning to nominate prognostic signatures and candidate therapeutic targets.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is multi-omic integration of public cohorts to derive candidate biomarkers. Most items combine bulk transcriptomics (often TCGA or GEO datasets) with single-cell data, WGCNA, and machine learning to produce gene signatures, such as the nine-gene HERC5-centered osteosarcoma risk score, the six-gene preeclampsia signature (AUC 0.869), and the 12-gene non-obstructive azoospermia signature (AUC 0.87). Proteomic evidence is less common: a paired hepatocellular carcinoma proteome and phosphoproteome study reports 314 altered proteins and 189 altered phosphopeptides, and a proteomics review addresses drug target discovery, toxicity, and resistance profiling. The newer contribution is the extension of these signatures to immune microenvironment and drug response readouts, with some items adding experimental validation such as IHC, siRNA knockdown, or in vitro assays. Many findings are exploratory or retrospective and remain to be validated in independent cohorts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-14"
}
